      "writtenOn": "2025-10-27T18:56:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The case where the commands time out is what I was worried about. There\u0027s nothing logically wrong with that, but it\u0027s hard to debug because the failure looks similar to what happens if the FPMCU is unresponsive for some reason.\n\n\u003e How about not checking in here. I will check before global_context.fp_frame_size_cache-\u003eget_frame_size is called that global_context.fp_frame_size_cache is not a nullptr.\n\nIt\u0027s kind of ugly if we have to repeat the check everywhere. If we say that a frame_size of 0 is an error, then we could modify the FpFrameSizeCache class to just return 0 when it doesn\u0027t have the value. We should make sure to add logging in the class when hitting that case and also make sure that a `frame_size` of 0 would be handled everywhere correctly. Since we\u0027re already initializing it to 0 at startup, I guess we already need that condition.\n\n(Note that if you make this change you no longer need the `Create` method)",
